This is a problem in linear algebra.  The lines are in the form $y=mx+b$, where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.  Two lines are parallel if and only if their slopes are equal.  Two lines are perpendicular if and only if the product of their slopes is $-1$.  Therefore, we can determine whether a pair of lines is good by checking whether the product of their slopes is $-1$ or whether their slopes are equal.  We can do this by forming a matrix with the slopes of the lines as the columns and the $y$-intercepts as the rows.  The matrix is

\begin{bmatrix}
3 & 4 & 9 & 1 & 1 \\
5 & 5 & -2 & -3 & -5
\end{bmatrix}

We can then use the determinant of this matrix to determine whether the lines are good.  If the determinant is 0, then the lines are either parallel or perpendicular.  If the determinant is not 0, then the lines are not good.  The determinant of the matrix is

\begin{align*}
\det\begin{bmatrix}
3 & 4 & 9 & 1 & 1 \\
5 & 5 & -2 & -3 & -5
\end{bmatrix}
&=3\cdot(5\cdot(-5)-(-3)\cdot(-2))-(4\cdot(5\cdot(-5)-(-3)\cdot(-2)))+(9\cdot(5\cdot(-5)-(-3)\cdot(-2)))-(1\cdot(5\cdot(-5)-(-3)\cdot(-2)))+(1\cdot(5\cdot(-5)-(-3)\cdot(-2)))\\
&=3\cdot(-55+6)-(4\cdot(-55+6))+(9\cdot(-55+6))-(1\cdot(-55+6))+(1\cdot(-55+6))\\
&=3\cdot(-49)-4\cdot(-49)+9\cdot(-49)-(-49)+(-49)\\
&=3\cdot(-49)+4\cdot(-49)+9\cdot(-49)+49+49\\
&=3\cdot(-49)+4\cdot(-49)+9\cdot(-49)+4

